Selectors in UiPath
1. What is a Selector?
A Selector in UiPath is a string of properties and attributes used to identify a specific user interface (UI) element in an application or website.
Selectors help UiPath identify the exact element that the automation needs to interact with, such as a button, text box, link, checkbox, or table.
For example, when UiPath needs to click a Login button, the selector helps UiPath identify the correct Login button on the screen.

2. Why are Selectors Used?
Selectors are mainly used to:
Identify UI elements.
Perform actions on specific elements.
Automate web and desktop applications.
Make automation more reliable.
Distinguish one UI element from other similar elements.

3. How Selectors Work
When a UI element is indicated in UiPath, UiPath identifies the element using its properties and attributes.
For example:
<html title='Amazon.in' />
<webctrl tag='INPUT' name='field-keywords' />
Here, attributes such as tag and name help UiPath identify the required element.

4. Common Selector Attributes
Some commonly used attributes are:
id – Identifies an element using its unique ID.
name – Identifies an element using its name.
class – Identifies the CSS class of an element.
tag – Specifies the type of HTML element.
title – Identifies an element using its title.
aaname – Represents the accessible name of an element.

5. Types of Selectors
5.1 Full Selector
A Full Selector contains the complete hierarchy of the UI element from the top-level application or browser element to the target element.
It provides detailed information for identifying the element.
5.2 Partial Selector
A Partial Selector contains only the necessary information required to identify the target element.
It is generally shorter and can be more flexible when the complete hierarchy is not required.

6. Static and Dynamic Selectors
Static Selector
A Static Selector contains attributes whose values remain the same when the application is opened or used.
Static selectors are generally more reliable.

Dynamic Selector
A Dynamic Selector contains attributes whose values may change during different sessions or executions.
Dynamic attributes can make automation less reliable if they are used directly.

7. Selector Example
Suppose a web page contains a search box:
<webctrl tag='INPUT' name='search' />
Here:
webctrl represents the web control.
tag='INPUT' identifies the HTML input element.
name='search' helps identify the specific search box.
UiPath uses these attributes to locate the required element.

8. Best Practices for Selectors
Prefer stable attributes such as id, name, or aaname when they are reliable.
Avoid unnecessary attributes.
Avoid attributes whose values change frequently.
Use UiPath's selector editor to inspect and modify selectors.
Test selectors before running the complete automation.
Use wildcards such as * when appropriate for dynamic values.

9. Importance of Selectors
Selectors are an important part of UiPath automation because they allow robots to reliably identify and interact with UI elements. A well-designed selector makes an automation workflow more stable and reduces errors caused by changes in the application interface.
Summary
Selector = A mechanism used by UiPath to identify and interact with a specific UI element.
